(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/doc_type "adr")
  (:adr/id "adr-2609111100-a-generator-that-cannot-derive-a-test-should-refuse-not-invent")
  (:adr/topic "q9-generator-no-refs")
  (:adr/title "A generator that cannot derive a test should refuse, not invent the input the test needs")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-10")
  (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Opus 5"))
  (:adr/repos (vector "com-junkawasaki/root" "kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang" "kotoba-lang/com-aave"))
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609111000-an-unchanged-oracle-is-the-control-for-a-regeneration"))
  (:adr/body
  "## Context

`com-aave` was the last pilot to check against a generated candidate. It was
chosen because it declares coercions on unrequired fields across three entities
-- more than any other pilot sampled -- so if the fixture gap closed last
iteration existed anywhere else, it should be widest there.

The generator never got that far.

## What happened

    kbb --backend sci gen-whole-component.cljk <template> <repo> aave
    Error: No item 0 in vector of length 0

All four aave specs are `:refs {}`. `ref-row` indexed the empty ref-field vector
and the run died. An index error is the worst available outcome: it names
nothing, writes nothing, and leaves the caller to guess whether the input, the
template or the generator was at fault.

The condition was already known to the code. `plan` computes `:refs-exercised?`
and the summary line already prints `expand folds over nothing in this
repository`. It simply was not honoured at the point where it mattered.

## Decision: refuse, and do not invent

The generator now refuses with the entity list and a hint, exits 2, and writes
nothing.

The tempting alternative is to generate something. The hand-written `com-aave`
component shows exactly what that something would look like, and it is good
work: it passes `expand` a SYNTHETIC ref table -- `\"liquidityIndex:ReserveData\"`
-- so the fold runs even though the real table yields nothing, and it keeps
selector 0 on the REAL table so the emptiness is documented rather than hidden.

A person weighed that and chose it. A generator doing the same on its own would
be manufacturing the very test input whose absence it exists to detect. The arms
would go green over a fold the production path never performs, and the green
would look exactly like the green from a repository where the fold really runs.

That is the distinction worth keeping: **a human may construct a test input and
say so; a generator that constructs one has removed the reader's ability to tell
a derived test from an invented one.**

## Reproduce

    # refuses, exit 2, writes nothing
    kbb --backend sci gen-whole-component.cljk <template> <com-aave> aave
    # still generates, exit 0
    kbb --backend sci gen-whole-component.cljk <template> <com-accela> accela

⚠ Read those exit codes from a file, not through a pipe. Measured again in this
iteration: `... | tail -3; echo exit=$?` printed `exit=0` for the refusing run,
because that is `tail`'s status. The refusal was on screen and the exit code
said the opposite.

## What this leaves unanswered

The question this iteration set out to ask -- does `com-aave`'s hand-written
coercion fixture carry the same gap the generator's did -- is still open, and
cannot be answered by generating a candidate. It has to be read directly. What
IS known is that its fixture is richer than a generated one would be: it carries
`\" 80 \"` with surrounding spaces to exercise trimming and `\"YES\"` to exercise a
case-insensitive bool, neither of which a placeholder generator produces.

`com-aadhaar` remains unexamined against a candidate.")))
